Reserve file-backed room after RELRO for the PS5 process parameters.

sce_module_writer places the process parameters and parameter blocks (about
0x340 bytes) directly after the RELRO content. It requires them to end before
the data segment, and it writes them at RELRO's file residue
(relro_file + address - relro_start). The linker script only left
page-alignment slack there, so the layout failed ("LLVM layout leaves no room
for PS5 process parameters") once the add-on export tables grew RELRO to
within 0x340 bytes of a page boundary.

A bare `. += N` gap is not enough. It adds address space but no file bytes,
and lld keeps the next segment's file offset tight. The parameters then
overwrote the preserved original .dynamic, and the in-process add-on resolver
rejected it ("original host dynamic metadata missing or invalid").

So reserve a zero-filled PROGBITS section in the RELRO segment. The converter
ignores it when classifying sections and overwrites its bytes with the
parameters. It also now fails packaging if the parameters would land on
another section's file bytes.

--- a/tooling/native/ps5-pie-base.ld	2026-10-14 23:30:02.071994252 +0200
+++ b/tooling/native/ps5-pie-base.ld	2026-10-14 23:30:27.820567229 +0200
@@ -64,6 +64,13 @@
     .rela.dyn : { *(.rela.dyn) } : relro
     .rela.plt : { *(.rela.plt) } : relro
 
+    /* File-backed room for the PS5 process parameters and their blocks
+     * (about 0x340 bytes), which the converter writes right after the RELRO
+     * content. A bare location-counter gap has no file bytes: lld would place
+     * the next segment's file offset inside it and the parameters would
+     * overwrite the preserved original .dynamic. */
+    .ps5.process_param_reserve : { BYTE(0); . += 0xfff; } : relro
+
     . = ALIGN(CONSTANT(MAXPAGESIZE));
     .dynamic : {
         *(.dynamic)
